## 9. ATTENDANCE INTELLIGENCE ENGINE

Build a transparent attendance analytics service.

Display the following separately:
- Total invited attendees.
- Invitation delivery status, when available.
- Total confirmed attendees.
- Declined attendees.
- Maybe responses.
- Awaiting responses.
- Expected attendance estimate.
- Conservative and high-attendance scenarios.
- Actual checked-in attendees.
- Outstanding discrepancies.

Do not confuse invited people, responding households and confirmed individual attendees.

Provide function-level and meal-period-level forecasts.

Initially implement a reliable rules-based forecast. It may use confirmed individual RSVPs, pending responses, historical attendance patterns when sufficient comparable data exists, recent cancellations and time remaining before the function.

Show the assumptions behind every estimate. If historical data is insufficient, use clearly labelled scenarios rather than pretending to have a trained predictive model.

Keep confirmed attendance separate from forecast attendance. Do not silently convert an unconfirmed guest into a confirmed attendee.

Where statistically justified, expose prediction intervals, calibration metrics and sample sizes. Evaluate forecasting quality against actual attendance after completed events.

Avoid training across customer organizations without explicit, appropriate authorization and privacy safeguards.

## 10. CATERING COST AND FOOD PLANNING

Create a dedicated catering intelligence workspace.

Support:
- Configurable meal periods.
- Vegetarian and non-vegetarian counts.
- Vegan, Jain and other configurable dietary categories.
- Allergies and special meal requirements.
- Adult and child meal categories.
- Different per-plate prices.
- Vendor minimum guarantees.
- Minimum billable quantities.
- Price tiers and optional service charges.
- Taxes and discounts where applicable.
- Final headcount deadlines.
- Authorized headcount revisions.
- Catering quotes and approved budgets.
- Planned quantities versus actual consumption.

Implement a transparent cost calculator.

For example:

Estimated catering cost = sum of applicable meal quantities multiplied by their configured rates, adjusted for contractual minimums, applicable charges and discounts.

Do not assume every attendee consumes one identical plate. Support event-specific meal eligibility, meal periods and vendor-defined billing rules.

Display:
- Cost at the invited headcount.
- Cost at confirmed attendance.
- Cost under conservative, expected and high-attendance scenarios.
- Potential cost exposure from uncertain attendance.
- Contractual minimum cost.
- Approved final catering commitment.
- Actual final invoice and reconciliation, when available.

Clearly distinguish a theoretical difference in food cost from genuine cash savings. Savings are realized only when the contractual arrangement and operational decisions allow a lower cost.

Allow authorized caterers to acknowledge a headcount and its applicable deadline. Maintain an audit trail for changes after approval.

Generate printable kitchen preparation sheets and downloadable PDF/CSV/XLSX reports as supported by the existing stack.

